  [1mThe fork:[0m what the logger does when sensitive data is passed to it. Today [38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m logs the username on every attempt,
  and the password sits one variable away from that call. Once a logger makes logging easy, the odds of a credential
  landing in it go up, so the mechanism matters more than the discipline.

  - [1mDeny-list by key name[0m — the logger walks any metadata object and replaces values under keys like [38;5;153mpassword[39m, [38;5;153mtoken[39m,
    [38;5;153msecret[39m with [38;5;153m[redacted][39m. Catches the realistic mistake, stays out of the way. Fails open: a sensitive field nobody
    added to the list gets logged in full.
  - [1mAllow-list by key name[0m — only explicitly permitted keys survive; everything else is redacted. Fails closed,
    genuinely safer. Costs a decision at every new log call, and in my experience that's the kind of friction people
    route around by stuffing data into the message string, which no key-based scheme can see.
  - [1mNo mechanism, convention only[0m — document "don't log secrets." Zero code. Relies entirely on whoever writes the next
    log call, including me.

  There's a second, separate question inside this: whether the [1musername[0m counts as sensitive. It's currently logged in
  cleartext and it's the thing you'd most want for correlating a user's report to their session — but it's also PII, and
  it's half of a credential pair. I'd suggest logging it under a key the deny-list leaves alone by default, and making
  that an explicit, visible choice in the spec rather than an accident inherited from the current code.

  [1mMy recommendation: deny-list by key name, plus a rule that the logger never accepts positional extra arguments — only
[0m  [1ma message and one metadata object.[0m The single-object shape is what makes redaction possible at all; a variadic
  [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m-alike can't inspect what it's given. Fails-open is the real cost, and I'd rather pay it than build an
  allow-list people bypass.
